@@ -91,6 +91,8 @@ The `context` object contains:
 - `retriesLeft` - Number of retries remaining
 - `retriesConsumed` - Number of retries consumed so far
 - `retryDelay` - The delay in milliseconds before the next retry (based on `minTimeout`, `factor`, `maxTimeout`, and `randomize`). The actual delay may be shorter if it would exceed `maxRetryTime`. This is `0` when no retry will occur based on the checks completed before the current callback runs. `shouldConsumeRetry` is the exception: it is shown the delay that would be used, since deciding whether to consume the retry is what it is being asked.
+- `retryDelay` also includes any wait requested by the error: if it has a numeric `retryAfter` (seconds, as in an HTTP `Retry-After` header), that is added to the computed backoff rather than replacing it, so many clients released by the same rate window do not all return at the same instant. Non-numeric, negative, or non-finite values are ignored. `maxRetryTime` still applies.
+- `elapsedTime` - Milliseconds elapsed since `pRetry` was called, measured when the context is created. It is not necessarily an integer, and it includes time spent in the operation, in callbacks, and waiting between retries.
 
